     1	# Model aliases and ccsession tiers
     2	
     3	Agents, commands, skills and the Python package pick models by the plain aliases
     4	`opus`, `sonnet` and `haiku`. Apart from the allow-list below, they never name a
     5	ccsession picker id such as `claude-gw-tier1a`. Inside a ccsession tier workspace the aliases map to tier
     6	models through three environment variables, which the launcher will set only in
     7	tier mode (see "The mapping" for the current status). Once that is in place,
     8	outside a tier workspace the launcher sets nothing and the aliases keep Claude
     9	Code's own meaning. Issue #384 records the decision.
    10	
    11	## What a primitive may name
    12	
    13	Except for the allow-list in the last bullet:
    14	
    15	- A frontmatter `model:` is `opus`, `sonnet`, `haiku` or `inherit`.
    16	- A `--model` or `--models` value in a command, skill or script is an alias.
    17	- A Python string constant that selects a model is an alias, never a dated
    18	  model id such as `claude-opus-4-8`.
    19	- Allow-list: the three issue workers pin picker ids on purpose, because they
    20	  must run on a fixed tier (`issue-worker-t3` on `claude-gw-tier3`,
    21	  `issue-worker-t2` on `claude-gw-tier2`, `issue-worker-t1` on
    22	  `claude-gw-tier1a`). Outside a ccsession tier workspace they stop with
    23	  `E-TIER-UNAVAILABLE`; there is no alias fallback. The `sc-issue-protocol` skill
    24	  and the `ccsession-tag` launcher package may also contain tier ids.
    25	
    26	`tests/skills/test_model_alias_policy.py` enforces this. It scans the agent,
    27	command and skill files and the Python sources, and it lists every exception
    28	with its reason.
    29	
    30	## The mapping
    31	
    32	Owner decision of 2026-11-05. Inside a ccsession tier workspace:
    33	
    34	| Alias | Tier model it resolves to | Set by |
    35	|---|---|---|
    36	| `opus` | `claude-gw-tier1a` | `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL=claude-gw-tier1a` |
    37	| `sonnet` | `claude-gw-tier1b` | `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL=claude-gw-tier1b` |
    38	| `haiku` | `claude-gw-tier2` | `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL=claude-gw-tier2` |
    39	
    40	Once the launcher applies the exports (status below), outside a ccsession tier
    41	workspace it sets none of the three, so `opus`, `sonnet` and `haiku` keep Claude
    42	Code's meaning. Today the workspace defaults file
    43	(`/etc/aidev02/aienv.d/defaults.sh`) sets all three, whether or not tier mode is
    44	on (there `haiku` resolves to `glm-5.3`).
    45	
    46	The exports are:
    47	
    48	```text
    49	ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL=claude-gw-tier1a
    50	ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL=claude-gw-tier1b
    51	ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL=claude-gw-tier2
    52	```
    53	
    54	Status: the ccsession launcher applies these exports in tier mode in a follow-up
    55	change, made after the #315 pull request merges. Until then the workspace
    56	defaults file (`/etc/aidev02/aienv.d/defaults.sh`) exports real model ids for the
    57	three variables, so the aliases resolve to those models and not to the tier ids.
    58	
    59	## Why aliases and not picker ids
    60	
    61	Claude Code resolves the model of a subagent in a fixed order (source:
    62	<https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents>, read 2026-11-05):
    63	
    64	- The Agent-call `model` parameter takes only the aliases `sonnet`, `opus`,
    65	  `haiku` and `fable`. It cannot carry a picker id, and it wins over the
    66	  agent's own `model:`.
    67	- The agent's frontmatter `model:` takes an alias, a full model id or `inherit`.
    68	  It is a literal value; the page documents no environment lookup there, so
    69	  `model: ${SOMETHING}` is not an option.
    70	- Next comes the `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` variable, then the main session's
    71	  model.
    72	- An alias is looked up in `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL`,
    73	  `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL` or `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL`, and those
    74	  variables may hold any id the gateway serves, tier ids included.
    75	
    76	The last point was checked on 2026-11-05 against the workspace gateway. See
    77	[the probe record](../../.dev/issues/384/analysis/probe-alias-remap.md): a
    78	subagent with `model: opus` ran on `claude-gw-tier1a` and one with
    79	`model: haiku` ran on `claude-gw-tier2`, while the main session kept its own
    80	model. Claude Code logs an `unrecognized_model` notice for each tier id but
    81	serves the request. That probe covered the frontmatter path and `--model`; it
    82	did not exercise the Agent-call parameter.
    83	
    84	## Known consequences
    85	
    86	- **Reflect reviewer diversity.** The reflect protocol counts how many distinct
    87	  model classes the three variables hold. Tier ids are three distinct strings, so
    88	  it sees three classes, but tier 1a and tier 1b both list Claude Sonnet 5.5
    89	  (`src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/MODEL-TIERS.md`), so the reviewers are
    90	  less different than the count suggests.
    91	- **Sprint alternate-model suggestion.** `suggest_alternate_model` in
    92	  `src/superclaude/cli/sprint/aienv.py` still works when the failed model is
    93	  given as an alias (`opus` suggests `sonnet`). When the failed model is a real
    94	  model id taken from a cooldown message, for example `claude-opus-5-5`, it
    95	  matches nothing while the slots hold tier ids and returns `None`
    96	  (`aienv.py` lines 111-128).
    97	- **Roadmap names stay.** Step ids such as `generate-opus-architect` and
    98	  `best_model` values in recommendations keep their names, because the aliases
    99	  they use do not change.
   100	- **Implement retry ladder.** `refs/retry.md` of `sc-implement-protocol` named
   101	  its slot variables with an underscore after the tier digit, which the
   102	  workspace does not export. It now uses the exported names `T3Model01`,
   103	  `T2Model01`, `T2Model02`, `T1Model01` and `T1Model02`, so the primary ladder
   104	  activates wherever the four rung variables are set and replaces the alias
   105	  fallback ladder. In the current workspace that puts a non-Claude model on
   106	  rung 1, GPT models on rungs 0 and 2, and the Claude top rung on `T1Model01`;
   107	  the advisers it picks are `claude-opus-5-5` and `gpt-6.1-sol`. The full
   108	  account is in the Task 5 notes of the issue #384 plan package under
   109	  `.dev/tasks/`.
